---
title: Compilation
summary: How a subgraph is lowered to a straight-line CompiledSequence that serves all four differentiation passes; which inputs are differentiable; what each pass collects at the leaves; cut compilation as partial derivatives at an interior frontier; the epoch-keyed cache; and slot liveness.
order: 6
---

Evaluating a model by walking its graph node by node would be slow. Skoro instead **compiles** the
part of the graph a query needs into a `CompiledSequence`: a flat, index-based list of steps, the
program of [Differentiable operations](../operations/#a-program).

## Lowering

`compile_backward_from(&targets)` collects the targets' ancestors, sorts them topologically, and
lowers each node to one `CompiledStep` writing one slot:

| Node | Step | In a pass |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Constant, observed | `Constant` | a value; never differentiated |
| Parameter | `Parameter` | a leaf: value from `points`; cotangents collected |
| Live stochastic | `Sampler` | a leaf: value from `points`, or drawn when generating; cotangents collected |
| Mapped, log-likelihood | `Operation` | the node's `DiffOperation`, or its cached value if it is constant-folded |
| A node named as a cut | `Given` | a leaf standing for an interior node (below) |

The same sequence runs all four passes (`forward`, `jvp_forward`, `vjp_forward`, `hvp_forward`), and
the caller picks one by which method it invokes.

## Which inputs are differentiable

Each `Operation` step records, per input, whether that input can carry a derivative. An input is
differentiable when its parent is a continuous parameter or live stochastic node, or a continuous
mapped or log-likelihood node that has not been constant-folded. Constants and observed nodes are
not. This is the `diffs` flag of [the two gates](../operations/#the-two-gates). An operation with
no differentiable input is kept off the tape entirely, so whole subtrees rooted in constants cost
one forward evaluation and nothing more.

## The four passes

With slots $s_0, \dots, s_{n-1}$, leaves $x$ and targets $f$, the passes compute the recurrences of
the operations page over the steps:

- **`forward`** runs every step: the values $f(x)$.
- **`jvp_forward`** takes a tangent frame $\dot X_x \in \R^{d_x \times k}$ per leaf (missing means
  zero) and returns, per target, $\sum_x \partial f / \partial x \cdot \dot X_x$. The Jacobian's
  JVP assembler seeds the columns of an identity.
- **`vjp_forward`** takes a cotangent frame $\alpha_f$ per target and returns, per leaf,
  $\sum_f (\partial f/\partial x)\transpose \alpha_f$.
- **`hvp_forward`** takes a tangent vector per leaf, and order-1 and order-2 cotangents per target,
  and returns per leaf the pair $(\alpha_x, \beta_x)$ of the forward-over-reverse recurrence: for a
  scalar target seeded $(1, 0)$, the gradient and the Hessian–vector product
  ([Manifolds and retractions](../manifolds/#the-hessianvector-product)).

Cotangents are collected **only at leaf slots** (`Parameter`, `Sampler`, `Given`). A cotangent
pulled into a constant is discarded before it can be observed. A leaf that no target reaches comes
back as absent, and the Jacobian assemblers read absent as zero.

## Cut compilation

`compile_between(&cuts, &targets)` stops the traversal at a frontier $c$ of interior nodes whose
values the caller supplies. The program then computes $f(x, c)$ with $c$ as an *independent*
input, and its derivatives are **partial** derivatives with the frontier held fixed:
$$
\frac{\partial f}{\partial c}\Big|_{x}, \qquad \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\Big|_{c}.
$$
The total derivative through the cut is the chain rule a caller can assemble from two programs:
$\frac{df}{dx} = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\big|_c + \frac{\partial f}{\partial c}\big|_x \frac{\partial c}{\partial x}$.
This is how a linearization at an interior node, such as $\partial h / \partial x_1$ for
$x_1 = F(x_0)$ in an all-mapped chain, is taken.

> [!WARNING] The trap
> A cut boundary must **not** lower to a constant step. If it did, everything downstream would be
> marked non-differentiable, and the values would come back right while the derivatives came back
> silently wrong. Cut nodes lower to a dedicated `Given` step: a leaf whose value is supplied and
> whose cotangent is collected, like a parameter.

A `Given` value comes from `points` (`MissingPoint` if it is absent) and is reported among the
output's `variables`, beside the parameters. A cut at a node that is already a leaf is a no-op, and
a cut that no target reaches is ignored. The Jacobian assemblers deliberately do not take cuts:
their columns are the leaves an estimator moves ([Jacobians](../jacobians/)).

`Graph::support(id)` is the forward counterpart: the nodes that depend on `id`, sorted for
determinism.

## Caching, keyed by epoch

Compiled sequences are cached. A target list alone is **not** a sound key: `observe`,
`demote_to_parameter`, `remove` and constant folding change a node's kind or value at a fixed `Id`,
which changes which inputs are differentiable, and so the compiled program. The key is
`(epoch, cuts, targets)`, and every graph mutator bumps the epoch, the `add_*` family included, so
the rule has no exceptions to remember.

- **Targets are keyed verbatim.** Their order fixes the step order and so every slot index, so
  `[a, b]` and `[b, a]` are different programs. Cuts are sorted and deduplicated, because only
  their membership matters.
- **The cache is bounded.** Past `MAX_CACHED_SEQUENCES` distinct keys in one epoch it clears
  itself rather than growing, and a cloned `Graph` starts with an empty cache.

Repeated evaluation without mutation (a Fisher-scoring loop, for example) benefits. A workload that
mutates every tick (a sliding window) clears the cache every tick and gains nothing. That is
expected.

## Slot liveness

Each operation slot is released at the last step that reads it, a fact computed at compile time
(`CompiledSequence::last_use`). Only operation slots are dropped. Leaf slots stay alive, because
the VJP pass reads them after the tape has run.

> [!NOTE]
> `docs/skoro.md` §4.5 summarizes the same design. The tests that pin each failure mode are in
> `crates/skoro-graph/tests/`: `compiler_cache.rs`, `cut_compilation.rs`, `slot_liveness.rs` and
> `signed_terms.rs`.
